-- [Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'X-ray crystallography of a folded protein shows that the backbone C–N bond linking each pair of adjacent residues is measurably shorter than a typical C–N single bond, and that the two atoms bonded to the carbonyl carbon and the two atoms bonded to the amide nitrogen all lie in a single plane with little rotation observed around that C–N bond. Which explanation best accounts for these observations?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The nitrogen lone pair delocalizes into the carbonyl, giving the C–N bond partial double-bond character."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Hydrogen bonds between each carbonyl oxygen and a nearby amide hydrogen lock the backbone atoms into one plane."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Steric clash between the two flanking side chains prevents any twisting around the C–N bond."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The nitrogen becomes sp-hybridized, forming a true C–N double bond in place of the single bond."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The peptide (amide) linkage that joins residues is not a simple C–N single bond: the nonbonding electron pair on the amide nitrogen delocalizes toward the carbonyl carbon, so the bond has partial double-bond character. This resonance forces the carbonyl carbon and amide nitrogen (and the two other atoms bonded to each) into one plane, gives them \(sp^2\) character with roughly 120° bond angles, shortens the C–N distance relative to a pure single bond, and restricts rotation about the bond (LibreTexts lines 10385–10388, 10501). (Choice A) is correct: delocalizing the nitrogen lone pair into the carbonyl produces the partial double-bond character that explains both the shortened C–N bond and its planarity and restricted rotation. (Choice B) is incorrect: backbone hydrogen bonds between a carbonyl oxygen and an amide hydrogen do form, but they stabilize higher-order secondary structure such as helices and sheets — they are not what confers the individual peptide bond''s own rigidity, planarity, or shortened length. (Choice C) is incorrect: side-chain (R-group) bulk restricts the \(\phi\) and \(\psi\) rotations around the alpha carbon that separates two peptide planes (LibreTexts lines 10404–10408), not rotation around the C–N bond itself, and it would not shorten that bond. (Choice D) is incorrect: the character is partial, not a full double bond, and the nitrogen adopts \(sp^2\) (not \(sp\)) hybridization consistent with its 120° geometry; describing it as a true double bond from \(sp\) nitrogen overstates the resonance effect. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the resonance model of the amide bond to interpret specific structural observations (a shortened bond, planarity, and restricted rotation) rather than recall a stated fact.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4cfc5d0d-fe18-4a6b-9dd7-cacb41453a50';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4cfc5d0d-fe18-4a6b-9dd7-cacb41453a50';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4cfc5d0d-fe18-4a6b-9dd7-cacb41453a50', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributes peptide-bond rigidity to hydrogen bonding (a real backbone feature) rather than to resonance'),
  ('4cfc5d0d-fe18-4a6b-9dd7-cacb41453a50', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'applies side-chain steric restriction of phi/psi angles to the wrong bond (the C–N bond itself)'),
  ('4cfc5d0d-fe18-4a6b-9dd7-cacb41453a50', 'D', 'misconception', 'upgrades partial double-bond character to a full double bond and misassigns sp hybridization');

-- [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] reengineer ans=B medium skill1
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A student sorts the 20 standard amino acids into "polar" and "nonpolar" bins using a simple structural rule: any side chain containing an O, N, or S heteroatom is called polar. This rule places serine, threonine, cysteine, and asparagine all in the polar bin. When the student later checks the Kyte-Doolittle hydropathy scale, one of these four residues has a strongly positive (hydrophobic) value and is, in fact, commonly found buried in the nonpolar interior of folded proteins. Which residue is it?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Serine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cysteine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Asparagine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Threonine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Structural classification bins a side chain as polar whenever it carries an O, N, or S heteroatom, but empirical hydropathy scales measure the actual free energy of transferring a side chain from water to a nonpolar solvent, and the two do not always agree. LibreTexts notes this discrepancy explicitly, stating that on the Kyte-Doolittle scale "cysteine is quite nonpolar" and that "cysteine is often buried in protein in a more hydrophobic environment," even though its -SH group leads the structural rule to classify it as polar (libretexts-biochemistry.txt, lines 5365-5366, 5237). (Choice A) is incorrect: serine''s -CH2OH side chain is genuinely hydrophilic, with a negative Kyte-Doolittle value (-0.8), so both the structural rule and the scale agree it is polar and surface-favoring. (Choice B) is correct: cysteine''s sulfhydryl (-SH) group makes it look polar structurally, but its Kyte-Doolittle value is strongly positive (+2.5, more hydrophobic than alanine at +1.8), so it is the residue whose empirical hydropathy contradicts the naive structural rule and which is commonly buried in the core. (Choice C) is incorrect: asparagine''s amide side chain is one of the most hydrophilic residues, with a strongly negative Kyte-Doolittle value (-3.5); structure and scale agree it is polar and surface-exposed. (Choice D) is incorrect: threonine''s hydroxyl side chain is hydrophilic, with a negative Kyte-Doolittle value (-0.7), so like serine it is correctly binned as polar by both the structural rule and the scale. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that cysteine, despite its polar-looking sulfhydryl group, is empirically hydrophobic on the Kyte-Doolittle scale and is frequently buried in the protein interior.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 1
WHERE id = 'ed218917-2f90-4d23-b87f-bf7f665ef8c3';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'ed218917-2f90-4d23-b87f-bf7f665ef8c3';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('ed218917-2f90-4d23-b87f-bf7f665ef8c3', 'A', 'misconception', 'heteroatom-implies-hydrophilic'),
  ('ed218917-2f90-4d23-b87f-bf7f665ef8c3', 'C', 'misconception', 'amide-looks-buriable'),
  ('ed218917-2f90-4d23-b87f-bf7f665ef8c3', 'D', 'misconception', 'hydroxyl-implies-hydrophilic');

-- [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher purifies a small single-domain enzyme and heats the solution until the enzyme completely loses its catalytic activity. When the solution is slowly cooled back to room temperature, with no chaperones or other proteins present, the enzyme spontaneously recovers essentially all of its original activity. Which conclusion about this enzyme is best supported by the result?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its native three-dimensional fold is specified by its amino acid sequence"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its activity depends on molecular chaperones that refold it after heating"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its peptide-bond backbone was cleaved by heat and then re-formed on cooling"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Its covalent structure was too stable for the heat to disrupt any bonding"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Heating disrupts the weak, noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds, hydrophobic contacts, van der Waals forces) that hold a protein in its native fold, producing an unfolded, inactive (denatured) state; the covalent primary structure — the peptide-bonded sequence of amino acids — is left intact. In Anfinsen''s classic ribonuclease experiment, the textbook notes that a denatured protein allowed to refold demonstrated that "the primary sequence contains all the information required for the protein to achieve its native conformation." (Choice A) is correct: because the enzyme refolds and regains full activity on its own once the denaturing heat is removed, the information needed to reach the active native fold must reside in the polypeptide itself — that is, in its amino acid sequence — since nothing else was present to guide folding. (Choice B) is incorrect: molecular chaperones help prevent misfolding and aggregation in the crowded cell, but this experiment contained no chaperones or other proteins, and the enzyme still refolded, so chaperones are not required here. (Choice C) is incorrect: heat denaturation disrupts noncovalent interactions, not the covalent peptide bonds of the backbone; the primary structure is not cleaved during simple thermal denaturation, so no bonds need to "re-form" on cooling. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enzyme did lose all activity when heated, which shows the native structure was in fact disrupted (unfolded); the covalent backbone survived, but the functional folded state clearly did not. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that a protein''s native fold is encoded by its primary sequence to interpret a spontaneous refolding result.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa', 'B', 'misconception', 'chaperones are always required for folding'),
  ('db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa', 'C', 'misconception', 'denaturation breaks covalent/primary structure'),
  ('db2b9f84-c963-460a-b120-64458d4b33aa', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'regained activity means never denatured');

-- [Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry] reengineer ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Nearly every amino acid found in proteins is an L-isomer and, when its absolute configuration is assigned using Cahn–Ingold–Prelog (CIP) priority rules, is designated (S) at the \(\alpha\)-carbon. One proteinogenic amino acid, however, is designated (R) despite having the identical L-spatial arrangement of its four substituents. Which amino acid is the exception?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Serine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cysteine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Methionine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Threonine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Every chiral proteinogenic amino acid shares the same L-arrangement at the \(\alpha\)-carbon, but the (R)/(S) label depends on the CIP priority order of the four substituents: \(-\text{NH}_3^+\), \(-\text{COO}^-\), the side chain, and \(-\text{H}\). For nearly all amino acids the nitrogen ranks first, the carboxyl carbon (bonded to O, O, O) ranks second, and the side-chain carbon ranks third, which yields (S). The label flips to (R) only when a side chain atom outranks oxygen at the first point of difference. (Choice B) is correct: cysteine''s \(-\text{CH}_2\text{SH}\) side chain places a sulfur atom (Z = 16) on the very first side-chain carbon; because sulfur outranks the oxygens of the carboxyl group, the side chain is promoted above \(-\text{COO}^-\), swapping priorities 2 and 3 and inverting the descriptor to (R) even though the physical L-arrangement is unchanged. (Choice A) is incorrect: serine''s \(-\text{CH}_2\text{OH}\) side chain also carries a heteroatom on the first carbon, but that atom is oxygen, so the side chain cannot outrank the triply-oxygenated carboxyl carbon; serine remains L-(S). (Choice C) is incorrect: methionine does contain sulfur, but it sits two carbons out in \(-\text{CH}_2\text{CH}_2\text{SCH}_3\); at the first side-chain carbon the atoms are C, H, H, which lose to the carboxyl carbon''s O, O, O, so methionine is L-(S). (Choice D) is incorrect: threonine has a second stereocenter in its \(-\text{CH(OH)CH}_3\) side chain, but its \(\alpha\)-carbon still ranks the carboxyl above the side chain and is designated (S). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply CIP priority rules to each side chain to determine which one promotes an atom above the carboxyl oxygens and thereby inverts the (R)/(S) descriptor.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586', 'A', 'misconception', 'heteroatom-on-first-carbon assumed to flip priority regardless of element'),
  ('5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586', 'C', 'misconception', 'presence of sulfur assumed sufficient without accounting for its position'),
  ('5982b835-e805-4f47-baf1-5c1282149586', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'extra stereocenter/hydroxyl conflated with R designation');

-- [Amino Acid Structure & Stereochemistry] reengineer ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Every standard amino acid used in proteins belongs to the L family, yet the chiral residues are not all assigned the same absolute configuration: all of them except L-cysteine are \(S\) at the α-carbon, while L-cysteine is instead assigned \(R\). The atoms bonded to the α-carbon are arranged in space in the same relative orientation for L-cysteine as for the other L-amino acids, such as L-serine; only the descriptor differs. What does the cysteine exception best illustrate about amino acid stereochemistry?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The L/D and \\(R\\)/\\(S\\) systems classify stereochemistry by different criteria, so one fixed spatial arrangement can correspond to either descriptor."}, {"label": "B", "text": "L-cysteine and L-serine are enantiomers of each other, which is why they are given opposite absolute-configuration descriptors."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The α-carbon of L-cysteine holds its substituents in the opposite spatial arrangement compared with the other L-amino acids."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Assigning an amino acid to the L family always requires first determining whether its α-carbon is \\(R\\) or \\(S\\)."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The L/D system is a relative descriptor that fixes the spatial arrangement of the α-carbon by reference to L-glyceraldehyde (the "CORN" orientation), whereas the \(R\)/\(S\) system is an absolute descriptor assigned from Cahn–Ingold–Prelog priorities, which rank substituents by atomic number. Because the two systems judge configuration by different criteria, a single fixed spatial arrangement can map to either \(R\) or \(S\): the textbook states that "L-cysteine is also (R)-cysteine," while "all the other L-amino acids are (S), but this reflects the human decision to give a sulfur atom a higher priority than a carbon atom and does not reflect a real difference in configuration" (LibreTexts 5419–5461). (Choice A) is correct: L/D and \(R\)/\(S\) are independent classification schemes, so cysteine can keep the same L spatial arrangement as the other residues yet receive the opposite CIP descriptor simply because its sulfur outranks the carboxyl carbon. (Choice B) is incorrect: L-cysteine and L-serine are different compounds with different side chains, not mirror images of the same molecule, so they are not enantiomers; enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror images of one identical structure. (Choice C) is incorrect: the stem specifies that the spatial arrangement is the same as the other L-amino acids, and the textbook stresses the differing descriptor "does not reflect a real difference in configuration," so the atoms are not oppositely arranged. (Choice D) is incorrect: the L designation is assigned by spatial comparison to L-glyceraldehyde independently of the \(R\)/\(S\) descriptor, which is precisely why L can correspond to either \(R\) or \(S\); the two systems are not derived one from the other. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the relative L/D system and the absolute CIP \(R\)/\(S\) system to interpret why one spatial configuration yields different descriptors.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'B', 'misconception', 'confusing_different_compounds_with_enantiomers'),
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'C', 'misconception', 'descriptor_change_implies_spatial_change'),
  ('2434f09f-b70c-408f-8396-bd484d105d84', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'L_depends_on_RS_assignment');

-- [Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'During stepwise peptide synthesis, a chemist attaches a bulky, unreactive protecting group to the α-amino group of a free amino acid and then adds this modified amino acid to a peptide chain that is being elongated one residue at a time. What is the most likely consequence for the modified amino acid?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "It can be joined to the chain, but no further residue can be attached beyond it."}, {"label": "B", "text": "It can be joined to the chain, and further residues can still be attached beyond it."}, {"label": "C", "text": "It cannot be joined to the chain, because its carboxyl group is no longer available."}, {"label": "D", "text": "It cannot be joined to the chain, because peptide bonds form only between two amino groups."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A peptide bond is an amide linkage formed by dehydration synthesis: the textbook states the reaction "connects the carboxylic acid of the upstream amino acid with the amine functional group of the downstream amino acid to form an amide linkage," in which "this carbonyl of the modified end serves as an electrophile in the attack of the amine nitrogen, a nucleophile." Thus every new bond requires a free carboxyl group (activated as the electrophile) on one partner and a free α-amino group (the nucleophile) on the other. Blocking the α-amino group removes only the nucleophile that would accept the next residue. (Choice A) is correct: the modified amino acid still has an available carboxyl group, so it can be coupled to the chain''s free amino end, but its own capped amino group can no longer act as the nucleophile to accept a subsequent residue, so elongation stops there. (Choice B) is incorrect: this ignores that the α-amino group is the nucleophile required for the next coupling; with it blocked, no additional residue can add beyond this one, which is precisely why protecting groups are used to control chain growth. (Choice C) is incorrect: the protecting group was placed on the amino group, not the carboxyl group, so the carboxyl remains available to form an amide bond with the chain''s amino end. (Choice D) is incorrect: a peptide bond forms between a carboxyl group and an amino group (yielding an amide), not between two amino groups, so this misstates the chemistry of the linkage. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the nucleophile/electrophile roles in peptide bond formation to predict how blocking one functional group changes which couplings can still occur.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '8c747741-9044-4bec-af0f-5537494c82a0';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '8c747741-9044-4bec-af0f-5537494c82a0';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('8c747741-9044-4bec-af0f-5537494c82a0', 'B', 'misconception', 'ignores the amino group''s role as the nucleophile needed for the next coupling'),
  ('8c747741-9044-4bec-af0f-5537494c82a0', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses which terminus was blocked (amino vs carboxyl)'),
  ('8c747741-9044-4bec-af0f-5537494c82a0', 'D', 'misconception', 'misremembers peptide bond as forming between two amino groups rather than a carboxyl and an amino group');

-- [Isoelectric Point (pI)] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Glutamate has three ionizable groups, whose \(\text{pK}_a\) values are listed in the table. Starting from a strongly acidic solution and raising the pH, the amino acid passes through a species with a net charge of zero before becoming net negative. Based on the table, the isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)) of glutamate is closest to which value?

| Ionizable group (glutamate) | \(\text{pK}_a\) |
| --- | --- |
| \(\alpha\)-\(\text{COOH}\) | 2.2 |
| side-chain \(\gamma\)-\(\text{COOH}\) | 4.3 |
| \(\alpha\)-\(\text{NH}_3^+\) | 9.7 |',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 3.25\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 5.95\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 7.0\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(\\text{pI} \\approx 5.4\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The textbook states that as pH rises from acidic to basic, ionizable groups deprotonate in order of increasing \(\text{pK}_a\), and the \(\text{pI}\) is found by "averaging the pKa values of the two groups closest to and on either side of" the net-neutral species. A protonated carboxyl carries charge 0 and becomes \(-1\) when deprotonated, while a protonated \(\alpha\)-amino group carries \(+1\) and becomes 0 when deprotonated. For glutamate you must track the net charge across pH and identify which two \(\text{pK}_a\) values flank the zero-charge form. Below 2.2 both carboxyls are neutral and the amino group is \(+1\) (net \(+1\)); deprotonating the \(\alpha\)-\(\text{COOH}\) at 2.2 gives net 0; deprotonating the side-chain \(\gamma\)-\(\text{COOH}\) at 4.3 gives net \(-1\). The net-zero species is therefore bracketed by the two lowest \(\text{pK}_a\) values. (Choice A) is correct: the zero-charge form is flanked by \(\alpha\)-\(\text{COOH}\) (2.2) below and \(\gamma\)-\(\text{COOH}\) (4.3) above, so \(\text{pI} = (2.2 + 4.3)/2 = 3.25\). (Choice B) is incorrect: averaging the two terminal groups \(\alpha\)-\(\text{COOH}\) and \(\alpha\)-\(\text{NH}_3^+\), \((2.2 + 9.7)/2 = 5.95\), ignores that glutamate''s acidic side chain deprotonates before the amino group, so these two \(\text{pK}_a\) values do not bracket the net-neutral species. (Choice C) is incorrect: averaging the two highest values, \((4.3 + 9.7)/2 = 7.0\), is the rule for a basic amino acid, but glutamate is acidic and its zero-charge form lies at the low-pH end. (Choice D) is incorrect: averaging all three \(\text{pK}_a\) values, \((2.2 + 4.3 + 9.7)/3 = 5.4\), is not how \(\text{pI}\) is defined; only the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values flanking the net-neutral form are averaged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply charge-state reasoning to order the deprotonation events, locate the net-zero species, and average only the two \(\text{pK}_a\) values that flank it.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1514b8b3-96e6-4005-b48e-be25618c81f1';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1514b8b3-96e6-4005-b48e-be25618c81f1';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1514b8b3-96e6-4005-b48e-be25618c81f1', 'B', 'misconception', 'always-average-the-two-termini'),
  ('1514b8b3-96e6-4005-b48e-be25618c81f1', 'C', 'misconception', 'acidic-treated-as-basic'),
  ('1514b8b3-96e6-4005-b48e-be25618c81f1', 'D', 'misconception', 'average-all-pKa-values');

-- [Side-Chain Classification] reengineer ans=B hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher is designing a metalloenzyme active site that requires one amino acid residue whose side chain can readily shuttle a proton — existing in an appreciable mix of both its protonated and deprotonated forms — under the near-neutral conditions of the cytoplasm (pH ≈ 7.4). She consults a table of side-chain pKa values: aspartate ≈ 3.9, histidine ≈ 6.5, lysine ≈ 10.5, and arginine ≈ 12.5. Which residue best meets her requirement?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Aspartate"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Histidine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Lysine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Arginine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Whether a side chain is protonated at a given pH depends on how its pKa compares with that pH: by the +2 rule, a group is roughly half protonated when \(\text{pH} = \text{pK}_\text{a}\), and it shifts to ~99% one form once the pH is about two units away from its pKa. A residue can act as a proton shuttle (general acid–base) only if a meaningful fraction of it sits in each ionization state at the working pH, which requires a side-chain pKa close to that pH. (Choice B) is correct: histidine''s imidazole side chain has a pKa of about 6.5, only ~0.9 units below physiological pH, so at pH 7.4 it exists as an appreciable mixture of protonated and deprotonated forms and can readily gain or donate a proton — the reason histidine is the residue most often found doing general acid–base chemistry in enzyme active sites. (Choice A) is incorrect: aspartate''s carboxyl side chain has a pKa near 3.9, roughly 3.5 units below pH 7.4, so it is essentially fully deprotonated (negatively charged) and cannot shuttle a proton at physiological pH. (Choice C) is incorrect: lysine''s ε-amino side chain has a pKa of about 10.5, roughly 3 units above pH 7.4, so it remains essentially fully protonated (positively charged) and does not readily give up its proton under these conditions. (Choice D) is incorrect: arginine''s guanidino side chain has a pKa of about 12.5 and is the most basic standard residue, but that high pKa means it stays fully protonated at physiological pH — being the "most basic" makes it a poorer, not better, proton shuttle at pH 7.4. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between side-chain pKa and ambient pH to predict which residue is significantly ionizable at physiological pH.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd458540f-9425-4e83-b1d7-3bcfed4e05b7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd458540f-9425-4e83-b1d7-3bcfed4e05b7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d458540f-9425-4e83-b1d7-3bcfed4e05b7', 'A', 'misconception', 'acidic-residue-confused-for-proton-shuttle'),
  ('d458540f-9425-4e83-b1d7-3bcfed4e05b7', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'basic-but-wrong-pKa-window'),
