-- [Primary Structure] reengineer ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher purifies an intact 40-residue polypeptide and attempts to determine its sequence by Edman degradation. Amino acid composition analysis confirms the protein is full-length and undegraded, yet the very first cycle of the procedure releases no identifiable PTH-amino acid. Which feature of the polypeptide best explains this result?

A. The α-amino group at the N-terminus is acetylated
B. The polypeptide exceeds the length that Edman degradation can sequence
C. The two cysteine residues in the chain form a single disulfide bond
D. The α-carboxyl group at the C-terminus is amidated',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The α-amino group at the N-terminus is acetylated"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The polypeptide exceeds the length that Edman degradation can sequence"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The two cysteine residues in the chain form a single disulfide bond"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The α-carboxyl group at the C-terminus is amidated"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Edman degradation determines primary sequence by reacting phenylisothiocyanate with an uncharged, free N-terminal α-amino group, then cleaving that residue as a phenylthiohydantoin (PTH)-amino acid without disrupting the remaining peptide bonds; the cycle repeats to read the sequence from the N-terminus toward the C-terminus. The reaction therefore depends entirely on the N-terminal amine being available to attack the reagent. (Choice A) is correct: acetylation of the N-terminal α-amino group leaves no free amine for phenylisothiocyanate to react with, so no PTH-amino acid is generated in the first cycle at all, exactly the observed failure; the textbook notes Edman "will not work if the N-terminus has been chemically modified (e.g., by acetylation or formation of pyroglutamic acid)." (Choice B) is incorrect: the length limit (~50–60 residues) caps how many cycles can be read reliably because derivatization is incomplete over many rounds, but at 40 residues the chain is within range and, more importantly, length would never prevent the first residue from being released. (Choice C) is incorrect: a disulfide bond makes it hard to assign disulfide positions and can complicate sequencing across the linkage, but it does not block the free N-terminal amine, so a first PTH-amino acid would still be produced. (Choice D) is incorrect: Edman degradation proceeds from the N-terminus, so a modification of the C-terminal α-carboxyl group is irrelevant to the first cycle; this choice confuses the direction in which Edman chemistry reads the chain. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the mechanism of N-terminal Edman chemistry to a novel experimental failure and infer which structural feature blocks the first sequencing cycle.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936', 'B', 'misconception', 'applies a real length limit to the wrong symptom (first-cycle failure vs. how far sequencing can proceed)'),
  ('1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936', 'C', 'partial_truth', 'cites a genuine Edman limitation (disulfides) that does not block the N-terminal reaction'),
  ('1528fdcc-1d62-4484-ac59-bab7f1d2b936', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'confuses N-terminal vs C-terminal directionality of Edman chemistry');

-- [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] reengineer ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher analyzes a water-soluble globular protein and uses the Kyte-Doolittle hydropathy scale, on which positive values indicate hydrophobic side chains and negative values indicate hydrophilic ones (Phe +2.8, Leu +3.8, Trp -0.9, Tyr -1.3). She notices that one aromatic residue — despite being routinely grouped with the "nonpolar aromatic" amino acids in introductory classifications — is repeatedly found on the solvent-exposed surface rather than in the buried hydrophobic core. Which residue is she most likely describing?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Tyrosine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Phenylalanine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Leucine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Alanine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A residue''s placement in a folded, water-soluble protein tracks its position on a hydropathy scale: a continuous stretch of high average hydrophobicity (positive Kyte-Doolittle values) tends to be buried in the interior away from water, while low-hydrophobicity (negative) residues tend to sit at the solvent-exposed surface (libretexts-biochemistry.txt lines 5367-5369). The textbook explicitly notes that although the aromatic amino acids are often labeled nonpolar, the Kyte-Doolittle scale actually shows that "the two large aromatic tyrosine and tryptophan... are more polar than nonpolar" (lines 5363-5365), a discrepancy driven for tyrosine by its ring hydroxyl, which "increases its reactivity and solubility compared to phenylalanine" (lines 5255-5256). (Choice A) is correct: tyrosine is an aromatic amino acid commonly grouped as nonpolar, yet its Kyte-Doolittle value is negative (-1.3), so it behaves as hydrophilic and is favored at the water-exposed surface rather than the buried core. (Choice B) is incorrect: phenylalanine is aromatic but its side chain is an unsubstituted benzene ring with a strongly positive Kyte-Doolittle value (+2.8), so it is genuinely hydrophobic and is preferentially buried in the core — it is not the mislabeled residue. (Choice C) is incorrect: leucine has the highest hydrophobicity of these options (+3.8) and is a classic core-forming residue, and it is aliphatic rather than aromatic, so it does not fit the "aromatic residue" description at all. (Choice D) is incorrect: alanine has a small nonpolar methyl side chain with a modestly positive value (+1.8); it is neither aromatic nor unexpectedly surface-seeking, so it is not the residue whose classification is misleading. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between hydropathy values and residue burial to recognize that an aromatic side chain with a negative Kyte-Doolittle value behaves as hydrophilic and localizes to the protein surface.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '972bcc49-2349-4f89-8494-a2fb635c8ef4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '972bcc49-2349-4f89-8494-a2fb635c8ef4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('972bcc49-2349-4f89-8494-a2fb635c8ef4', 'B', 'misconception', 'assumes all aromatic residues are equally hydrophobic and buried'),
  ('972bcc49-2349-4f89-8494-a2fb635c8ef4', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'picks the most hydrophobic residue as the ''surprising'' one, ignoring the aromatic requirement'),
  ('972bcc49-2349-4f89-8494-a2fb635c8ef4', 'D', 'partial_truth', 'recognizes a nonpolar residue but it is neither aromatic nor surface-seeking');

-- [Conjugated Proteins & Denaturation] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A single-chain enzyme is unfolded by incubation in 8 M urea, which disrupts the noncovalent interactions that stabilize its folded shape. When the urea is later removed by dialysis, the enzyme spontaneously refolds and recovers full catalytic activity, even though no other proteins are added and its amino acid sequence never changes. This result most strongly supports which conclusion about the enzyme''s three-dimensional structure?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The native fold is dictated by the protein''s amino acid sequence."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The native fold requires peptide bonds to be broken and reformed."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The native fold depends on chaperone proteins supplied during refolding."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The native fold is held together mainly by covalent bonds between distant residues."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Denaturation disrupts the noncovalent interactions and higher-order structure that give a protein its native fold, but it leaves the covalent peptide backbone (the amino acid sequence) intact. In Anfinsen''s classic experiment, a denatured single-chain protein that spontaneously refolds and regains activity once the denaturant is removed demonstrates that "all the necessary and sufficient information required to direct the folding of a protein into the native state is present in the primary sequence" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 13813-13814). (Choice A) is correct: because the enzyme refolds spontaneously to its active shape with an unchanged sequence and no external machinery, the fold must be encoded by the sequence itself, exactly the inference Anfinsen drew from reversible denaturation/renaturation (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 13813-13833). (Choice B) is incorrect: denaturation and refolding disrupt and re-form noncovalent interactions, not the covalent peptide bonds; the backbone sequence "never changes," so peptide bonds are neither broken nor reformed during folding. (Choice C) is incorrect: while chaperones assist folding in vivo, this in vitro experiment adds no other proteins, so spontaneous recovery of activity shows the sequence alone suffices under these conditions (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 13847-13849). (Choice D) is incorrect: the fold is stabilized chiefly by noncovalent interactions and the hydrophobic effect, described as "the main thermodynamic driving force" for burying nonpolar residues (LibreTexts Biochemistry, line 4751), rather than by covalent bonds linking distant residues. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that denaturation disrupts noncovalent higher-order structure while preserving the primary sequence to infer, from a described reversible-refolding experiment, that the amino acid sequence determines the native fold.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '9a584f44-5e1a-4859-9510-668906f8953b';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '9a584f44-5e1a-4859-9510-668906f8953b';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('9a584f44-5e1a-4859-9510-668906f8953b', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses denaturation with breaking covalent peptide bonds'),
  ('9a584f44-5e1a-4859-9510-668906f8953b', 'C', 'misconception', 'assumes folding always requires chaperones'),
  ('9a584f44-5e1a-4859-9510-668906f8953b', 'D', 'misconception', 'attributes fold stability to covalent rather than noncovalent interactions');

-- [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] reengineer ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A typical aspartate side chain exposed to solvent on a protein''s surface has a carboxyl pKa near 4. In one enzyme, however, an aspartate residue packed into the protein''s hydrophobic core is measured to have a pKa near 9, meaning it stays protonated (neutral) across most of the physiological pH range. Which effect of the nonpolar microenvironment best explains this elevated pKa?

A. The nonpolar surroundings destabilize the negatively charged carboxylate, favoring the neutral protonated form.
B. The nonpolar surroundings stabilize the negatively charged carboxylate, favoring the deprotonated form.
C. The nonpolar surroundings physically block water from reaching and deprotonating the side chain.
D. The nonpolar surroundings lower the local pH, keeping the side chain protonated.',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "The nonpolar surroundings destabilize the negatively charged carboxylate, favoring the neutral protonated form."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The nonpolar surroundings stabilize the negatively charged carboxylate, favoring the deprotonated form."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The nonpolar surroundings physically block water from reaching and deprotonating the side chain."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The nonpolar surroundings lower the local pH, keeping the side chain protonated."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The pKa of an ionizable side chain is not fixed; it depends on the microenvironment, because the environment determines how stable the charged (deprotonated) form is relative to the neutral (protonated) form. A carboxyl group deprotonates readily when water and nearby polar or positively charged groups can stabilize the resulting negative charge, but a hydrophobic core offers none of that stabilization. (Choice A) is correct: buried among nonpolar groups, the aspartate carboxylate has no polar solvent or counter-charge to stabilize its negative charge, so the deprotonated \(-\text{COO}^-\) form is energetically unfavorable and the neutral \(-\text{COOH}\) form is favored; making a group harder to deprotonate raises its pKa, exactly as the textbook describes for a buried Asp whose pKa is elevated to about 9. (Choice B) is incorrect: this reverses the effect. A nonpolar environment cannot stabilize a full negative charge (it lacks the polar or ionic partners that do so), and stabilizing the deprotonated form would instead lower the pKa, not raise it. (Choice C) is incorrect: a shifted pKa is a thermodynamic property reflecting the relative stability of the two protonation states, not a kinetic barrier; the core does not work by mechanically shielding the proton from water, and burial does not prevent proton exchange. (Choice D) is incorrect: pH is a bulk property of the aqueous solution, not something a patch of nonpolar side chains sets to a different value; the microenvironment shifts the residue''s pKa, but it does not create a local pH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that a hydrophobic microenvironment destabilizes a charged side chain, and reason that favoring the neutral protonated form raises the measured pKa.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'cfe53a9a-ebd4-4433-9aa0-735ca9249c72';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'cfe53a9a-ebd4-4433-9aa0-735ca9249c72';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('cfe53a9a-ebd4-4433-9aa0-735ca9249c72', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'flips the direction of charge stabilization, so it predicts a lowered rather than raised pKa'),
  ('cfe53a9a-ebd4-4433-9aa0-735ca9249c72', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats a thermodynamic pKa shift as a kinetic/steric barrier to deprotonation'),
  ('cfe53a9a-ebd4-4433-9aa0-735ca9249c72', 'D', 'misconception', 'confuses microenvironment polarity with a local change in bulk-solution pH');

-- [Zwitterions & Titration Curves] reengineer ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Glutamate has three ionizable groups: the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 2.2\)), the side-chain \(\gamma\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 4.25\)), and the \(\alpha\text{-NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 9.7\)). A biochemist dissolves free glutamate in a buffer held at physiological \(\text{pH} = 7.4\). What is the net charge on the predominant form of glutamate under these conditions?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "\\(-1\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "\\(0\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "\\(-2\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "\\(+1\\)"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The net charge on an amino acid at a given pH is found by evaluating each ionizable group against its \(\text{pK}_a\): when the pH is above a group''s \(\text{pK}_a\) the group is deprotonated, and when the pH is below its \(\text{pK}_a\) the group is protonated (LibreTexts states that at pH two units above the \(\text{pK}_a\) a group is ~99% deprotonated, and two units below it is ~99% protonated, lines 5505-5510). Carboxyl groups carry \(0\) charge when protonated and \(-1\) when deprotonated, whereas the \(\alpha\)-amino group carries \(+1\) when protonated and \(0\) when deprotonated (lines 5485-5486, 5561). At \(\text{pH} = 7.4\): both the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 2.2\)) and the side-chain \(\gamma\text{-COOH}\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 4.25\)) are well above their \(\text{pK}_a\) values, so both are deprotonated at \(-1\) each; the \(\alpha\text{-NH}_3^+\) (\(\text{pK}_a = 9.7\)) is below its \(\text{pK}_a\), so it stays protonated at \(+1\). (Choice A) is correct: summing the charges gives \(-1 + (-1) + (+1) = -1\). (Choice B) \(0\) is incorrect: this treats glutamate as a simple neutral zwitterion, ignoring that its acidic side-chain carboxyl is deprotonated (\(-1\)) at pH 7.4; only amino acids without a charged R-group are net-neutral at their \(\text{pI}\). (Choice C) \(-2\) is incorrect: this deprotonates all three groups, but pH 7.4 is more than two units below the amino \(\text{pK}_a\) of 9.7, so the \(\alpha\)-amino group remains protonated at \(+1\) and is not removed. (Choice D) \(+1\) is incorrect: this counts only the protonated amino group and overlooks that both carboxyls are deprotonated at this pH, each contributing \(-1\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between pH and \(\text{pK}_a\) to each of the three ionizable groups and sum their individual charges to predict the net charge.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '42419bb7-8672-40fe-95cc-4455cbb73a87';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '42419bb7-8672-40fe-95cc-4455cbb73a87';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('42419bb7-8672-40fe-95cc-4455cbb73a87', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats amino acid as neutral zwitterion and ignores charged acidic side chain'),
  ('42419bb7-8672-40fe-95cc-4455cbb73a87', 'C', 'misconception', 'deprotonates the alpha-amino group at neutral pH, misapplying the pH-vs-pKa rule'),
  ('42419bb7-8672-40fe-95cc-4455cbb73a87', 'D', 'misconception', 'counts only the amino group and forgets both carboxyls deprotonate above their pKa');

-- [Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher chemically links 15 free amino acids into a single, unbranched linear peptide. Assuming each linkage forms by the same reaction that joins amino acids in a protein, how many water molecules are released during the synthesis of this peptide?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "14"}, {"label": "B", "text": "15"}, {"label": "C", "text": "16"}, {"label": "D", "text": "30"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Amino acids are joined into peptides by dehydration synthesis (condensation): the amine of one amino acid attacks the carbonyl carbon of another''s carboxyl group, forming an amide (peptide) bond and releasing one molecule of water per bond, as the textbook states that in forming the amide link "water is released." In an unbranched chain of \(n\) residues, the residues are connected in a single line, so the number of peptide bonds is \(n-1\); each of those bonds releases exactly one water molecule. (Choice A) is correct: 15 residues joined in a line require \(15-1=14\) peptide bonds, and because each bond formation releases one water, 14 water molecules are released. (Choice B) is incorrect: counting one water per residue (15) rather than one water per bond ignores that a linear chain of 15 residues has only 14 linkages, since the two terminal residues each participate in one bond, not two. (Choice C) is incorrect: 16 would count one more linkage than exists, as if the chain were cyclized (joining the ends adds a bond) or as if there were one bond per residue plus an extra; the peptide here is linear and unbranched, so no closing bond forms. (Choice D) is incorrect: doubling to 30 treats each peptide bond as though it released two waters, but the condensation of a carboxyl and an amine group releases only a single water per amide bond formed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the dehydration-synthesis mechanism of peptide bond formation to determine that a linear chain of \(n\) amino acids forms \(n-1\) bonds and therefore releases \(n-1\) water molecules.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd5db305f-d6ac-4e59-9fc1-d0b62ae1dfd4';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd5db305f-d6ac-4e59-9fc1-d0b62ae1dfd4';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d5db305f-d6ac-4e59-9fc1-d0b62ae1dfd4', 'B', 'misconception', 'off-by-one: counts one water per residue instead of per bond'),
  ('d5db305f-d6ac-4e59-9fc1-d0b62ae1dfd4', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats linear chain as cyclic / adds an extra linkage'),
  ('d5db305f-d6ac-4e59-9fc1-d0b62ae1dfd4', 'D', 'misconception', 'double-counts water per amide bond');

-- [Primary Structure] reengineer ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher synthesizes the linear pentapeptide Ala-Gly-Val-Leu-Phe, drawn in the conventional N-to-C direction. None of the five side chains in this peptide contains an ionizable group. Considering only the backbone \(\alpha\)-amino and \(\alpha\)-carboxyl groups, how many of them can gain or lose a proton to carry a charge at an appropriate pH?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "2 groups"}, {"label": "B", "text": "4 groups"}, {"label": "C", "text": "5 groups"}, {"label": "D", "text": "10 groups"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' In a polypeptide, each peptide bond ties up the \(\alpha\)-carboxyl group of one residue and the \(\alpha\)-amino group of the next residue in an amide linkage; the textbook states that "when the amino acids are linked by peptide bonds (amide link), the alpha N and the carboxyl C are in an amide link and are not charged," and that only "the amino group of the N-terminal amino acid and the carboxyl group of the C-terminal amino acid of a protein may be charged" (LibreTexts 3.1.10). Because every internal \(\alpha\)-group is locked in a peptide bond, only the free \(\alpha\)-amino group at the N-terminus and the free \(\alpha\)-carboxyl group at the C-terminus remain ionizable. (Choice A) is correct: with no ionizable side chains, the only backbone groups that can carry charge are the single free N-terminal \(\alpha\)-amino group and the single free C-terminal \(\alpha\)-carboxyl group, for a total of 2. (Choice B) is incorrect: counting the four internal \(\alpha\)-groups (or the four amide nitrogens) treats peptide-bonded backbone groups as titratable, but those \(\alpha\)-N and \(\alpha\)-C atoms are consumed in amide bonds and cannot gain or lose a proton. (Choice C) is incorrect: assigning one ionizable backbone group per residue ignores that in a chain of five residues four \(\alpha\)-amino and four \(\alpha\)-carboxyl groups are already committed to the four peptide bonds. (Choice D) is incorrect: this counts both the \(\alpha\)-amino and \(\alpha\)-carboxyl group of all five residues as free, which is only true for isolated amino acids; peptide bond formation removes all but the two terminal groups from the pool of ionizable backbone groups. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that peptide bonds convert internal \(\alpha\)-amino and \(\alpha\)-carboxyl groups into non-ionizable amide linkages to determine how many backbone groups remain titratable in a specific peptide.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6053e3f1-a02f-4e83-9295-ef57e07187cb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6053e3f1-a02f-4e83-9295-ef57e07187cb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6053e3f1-a02f-4e83-9295-ef57e07187cb', 'B', 'misconception', 'treats internal backbone groups as titratable'),
  ('6053e3f1-a02f-4e83-9295-ef57e07187cb', 'C', 'misconception', 'one ionizable group per residue'),
  ('6053e3f1-a02f-4e83-9295-ef57e07187cb', 'D', 'misconception', 'applies free-amino-acid charge count to a peptide');

-- [Zwitterions & Titration Curves] reengineer ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A biochemist titrates glutamate, an amino acid whose carboxylic acid side chain adds a third ionizable group. The three pK_a values are listed below.

| Ionizable group | \(\text{pK}_a\) |
|---|---|
| \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) | 2.2 |
| side-chain \(\gamma\text{-COOH}\) | 4.2 |
| \(\alpha\text{-NH}_3^+\) | 9.7 |

As base is added, the molecule passes through a species that carries a net charge of zero (the zwitterion). What is the isoelectric point (\(\text{pI}\)) of glutamate?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "3.2"}, {"label": "B", "text": "5.4"}, {"label": "C", "text": "6.0"}, {"label": "D", "text": "7.0"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' The isoelectric point is the pH at which the molecule carries no net charge; for a molecule with more than two ionizable groups it is found not by averaging the extreme or all pK_a values, but by averaging the two pK_a values that flank the net-zero (zwitterionic) species. To locate that species you track the charge as pH rises: at very low pH glutamate is fully protonated with net charge \(+1\) (both carboxyls neutral, amino group \(+1\)); deprotonating the \(\alpha\text{-COOH}\) (pK_a 2.2) gives net \(0\); deprotonating the side-chain \(\gamma\text{-COOH}\) (pK_a 4.2) gives net \(-1\); and deprotonating the \(\alpha\text{-NH}_3^+\) (pK_a 9.7) gives net \(-2\). The zwitterion therefore sits between pK_a 2.2 and pK_a 4.2, so \(\text{pI} = (2.2 + 4.2)/2 = 3.2\). (Choice A) is correct: 3.2 averages the two lowest pK_a values, which are the ones flanking the net-zero form of an acidic amino acid. (Choice B) is incorrect: 5.4 comes from averaging all three pK_a values, \((2.2 + 4.2 + 9.7)/3\), but the pI is defined only by the two that bracket the neutral species. (Choice C) is incorrect: 6.0 comes from averaging the two extreme values, \((2.2 + 9.7)/2\), the correct method for a simple amino acid with no ionizable side chain but wrong here because it skips the acidic \(\gamma\)-carboxyl and lands on a pH where the molecule is net \(-1\). (Choice D) is incorrect: 7.0 comes from averaging the two highest values, \((4.2 + 9.7)/2\); those pK_a values flank the transition from \(-1\) to \(-2\), not the net-zero species, so they give the wrong pH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of the isoelectric point to a triprotic amino acid, track its net charge through each deprotonation, and identify the correct pair of pK_a values to average rather than defaulting to a memorized two-value formula.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '77c9dedc-f49d-4b12-9c6a-c9ef5b066faf';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '77c9dedc-f49d-4b12-9c6a-c9ef5b066faf';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('77c9dedc-f49d-4b12-9c6a-c9ef5b066faf', 'B', 'misconception', 'average-all-pKa'),
  ('77c9dedc-f49d-4b12-9c6a-c9ef5b066faf', 'C', 'misconception', 'two-value-formula-ignores-side-chain'),
