-- [Primary Structure] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A tetrapeptide is written in standard convention as Val–Thr–Cys–Arg. Which residue retains a free α-amino group that is not incorporated into a peptide bond?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Valine"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Threonine"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Cysteine"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Arginine"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' By convention, a protein''s primary structure is written from the N-terminus to the C-terminus, so the leftmost residue in a sequence is the amino-terminal residue. Each peptide bond is an amide link formed between the α-carboxyl group of one residue and the α-amino group of the next; consequently, every residue''s α-amino group is consumed in a peptide bond except that of the very first residue, whose α-amino group remains free. (Choice A) is correct: valine is written first, marking it as the N-terminal residue, and only its α-amino group is not tied up in a peptide bond. (Choice B) is incorrect: threonine is the second residue, so its α-amino group forms the amide bond with valine''s α-carboxyl and is therefore not free. (Choice C) is incorrect: cysteine is an internal residue whose α-amino group is bonded to threonine''s carbonyl; a free α-amino group cannot appear in the interior of a chain. (Choice D) is incorrect: arginine is the C-terminal residue, so it retains a free α-carboxyl group, not a free α-amino group — a free α-amino group is a property of the N-terminus, and arginine''s nitrogen-rich guanidino side chain does not change which α-group is free. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the N-to-C writing convention and the mechanism of peptide-bond formation to deduce which single residue''s α-amino group escapes incorporation into an amide linkage.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '1cadec9c-8372-415c-a06c-b4067208d2ce';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '1cadec9c-8372-415c-a06c-b4067208d2ce';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('1cadec9c-8372-415c-a06c-b4067208d2ce', 'B', 'misconception', 'internal residue mistaken for having a free terminal group'),
  ('1cadec9c-8372-415c-a06c-b4067208d2ce', 'C', 'misconception', 'internal residue mistaken for having a free terminal group'),
  ('1cadec9c-8372-415c-a06c-b4067208d2ce', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'C-terminus confused with N-terminus / side-chain nitrogen mistaken for free alpha-amino');

-- [Secondary Structure (α-helix & β-sheet)] reengineer ans=C hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher studies a 21-residue segment of a membrane-associated protein. Charting the sequence, she finds that nonpolar residues occur at positions 1, 4, 8, 11, 15, and 18, while charged and polar residues occupy the intervening positions. When the segment folds, the nonpolar side chains all cluster along a single continuous longitudinal face of the folded segment, leaving the opposite face polar. Which secondary structure best accounts for this segregation of nonpolar side chains onto one face?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "A parallel \\(\\beta\\)-pleated sheet"}, {"label": "B", "text": "A random coil"}, {"label": "C", "text": "An \\(\\alpha\\)-helix"}, {"label": "D", "text": "A collagen triple helix"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'C',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Because the textbook notes an \(\alpha\)-helix has about 3.6 residues per turn (\(n = 3.6\)), residues spaced roughly three to four apart in the sequence end up rotated back onto nearly the same side of the helix; when hydrophobic residues recur at that spacing they collect on one longitudinal face, producing an amphipathic helix with "hydrophilic residues... on one side of the helix and hydrophobic residues on the other." Here the nonpolar residues sit at positions 1, 4, 8, 11, 15, 18 — a repeat of about 3.5 residues — exactly the periodicity that maps them onto a single face of a 3.6-residue-per-turn coil. (Choice A) is incorrect: in a \(\beta\)-strand successive side chains alternate to opposite faces, so an amphipathic \(\beta\)-strand shows a two-residue (i, i+2) hydrophobic repeat, not the ~3.5-residue repeat described here. (Choice B) is incorrect: a random coil lacks a regular repeating backbone geometry, so it cannot systematically project a defined set of positions onto one continuous face. (Choice C) is correct: only a coil with ~3.6 residues per turn converts a ~3.5-residue sequence periodicity into a single hydrophobic face. (Choice D) is incorrect: the collagen triple helix is a rope of three separate polyproline-II-like strands whose defining feature is a Gly-X-Y repeat and interchain packing, not the segregation of one strand''s side chains onto a hydrophobic face. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the ~3.6-residue-per-turn geometry of secondary structures to a sequence periodicity in order to infer which structure places the nonpolar side chains on one face.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '3c0fe327-9ef4-4d56-9f3a-7c31aba085c7';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '3c0fe327-9ef4-4d56-9f3a-7c31aba085c7';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('3c0fe327-9ef4-4d56-9f3a-7c31aba085c7', 'A', 'misconception', 'confuses helix periodicity with beta-strand alternation'),
  ('3c0fe327-9ef4-4d56-9f3a-7c31aba085c7', 'B', 'misconception', 'believes side-chain segregation needs no regular backbone geometry'),
  ('3c0fe327-9ef4-4d56-9f3a-7c31aba085c7', 'D', 'adjacent_fact', 'recognizes ''helix'' keyword but ignores triple-strand Gly-X-Y definition');

-- [Quaternary Structure & Cooperativity] reengineer ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A researcher compares two oxygen-carrying proteins from the same organism. Protein X is a single polypeptide with one heme site and gives a hyperbolic oxygen-saturation curve. Protein Y is an assembly of four subunits, each with one heme site, and gives a sigmoidal oxygen-saturation curve that rises steeply over a narrow range of oxygen pressures. Which statement best accounts for the sigmoidal curve observed for Protein Y?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Binding of oxygen at one subunit raises the oxygen affinity of the remaining subunits"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Each subunit binds oxygen with an identical, unchanging affinity independent of the others"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Oxygen must occupy all four subunits simultaneously before any binding can be measured"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Binding of oxygen at one subunit lowers the oxygen affinity of the remaining subunits"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A sigmoidal oxygen-binding curve is the signature of positive cooperativity in a multi-subunit protein: the textbook states that for hemoglobin, plots of fractional saturation versus oxygen pressure are ''sigmoidal for Hb, suggesting cooperative binding of oxygen to Hb (binding of the first oxygen facilitates binding of the second, etc)'' (line 20392), and that ''the binding of one oxygen molecule increases the affinity of the remaining sites (homotropic allosterism)'' (line 20980). A single-site monomer like myoglobin cannot show this behavior and instead gives a hyperbolic curve. (Choice A) is correct: cooperativity means that oxygen binding at one subunit shifts the tetramer toward a higher-affinity conformation, so the remaining subunits bind more readily — this produces the steep, sigmoidal rise. (Choice B) is incorrect: if every subunit bound with a fixed, independent affinity, the curve would be hyperbolic like Protein X''s, not sigmoidal, so identical unchanging affinity cannot explain the sigmoid. (Choice C) is incorrect: cooperativity does not require all four sites to fill simultaneously; oxygens bind sequentially, and partially saturated intermediates are measurable — an all-or-none requirement describes a hypothetical limit, not the graded sigmoidal binding seen here. (Choice D) is incorrect: if binding at one subunit lowered the affinity of the others (negative cooperativity), the remaining sites would resist further binding and the curve would flatten early rather than steepen, which does not match a sigmoidal curve. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between quaternary structure, cooperative binding, and curve shape to infer the mechanism behind an observed sigmoidal binding curve.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '4e561080-1af0-495a-ac2b-20214250af80';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '4e561080-1af0-495a-ac2b-20214250af80';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('4e561080-1af0-495a-ac2b-20214250af80', 'B', 'misconception', 'confuses independent binding (hyperbolic) with cooperative binding (sigmoidal)'),
  ('4e561080-1af0-495a-ac2b-20214250af80', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats cooperativity as all-or-none simultaneous occupancy rather than graded sequential binding'),
  ('4e561080-1af0-495a-ac2b-20214250af80', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'attributes sigmoidal curve to negative cooperativity instead of positive cooperativity');

-- [Tertiary Structure & Folding] reengineer ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A soluble globular enzyme buries a valine residue deep in its water-excluded core, packed against other nonpolar side chains. Using site-directed mutagenesis, a researcher replaces this buried valine with glutamate, whose side chain is negatively charged at physiological pH. The mutant enzyme folds but unfolds at a markedly lower temperature than the wild type, indicating a less stable tertiary structure. Which factor best accounts for the mutant''s reduced stability?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Placing the charged glutamate side chain in the nonpolar interior is energetically costly and weakens the hydrophobic packing of the core."}, {"label": "B", "text": "The glutamate side chain forms a disulfide crosslink with a neighboring residue that distorts the folded core."}, {"label": "C", "text": "The substitution breaks a backbone amide-to-carbonyl hydrogen bond that defined the local secondary structure."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The added surface charge raises the enzyme''s solubility, and greater solubility drives the polypeptide to unfold."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Tertiary structure is the overall 3D shape of a single polypeptide, and its dominant stabilizing force is the hydrophobic effect: nonpolar R groups are buried in a water-excluded interior while polar and charged R groups face the solvent. LibreTexts notes that nonpolar side chains such as Val, Leu, Ile, Met, and Phe are ''predominantly (83%) in the protein''s interior'' and that the hydrophobic effect is ''the main thermodynamic driving force'' for folding; it also cites staphylococcal nuclease mutants in which a buried Val is changed to a charged residue, imposing an energetic penalty for dehydrating and burying that charge. (Choice A) is correct: forcing a charged glutamate into the nonpolar, water-excluded core is energetically unfavorable because the charge is stripped of its stabilizing hydration and no longer participates in favorable hydrophobic packing, so the fold is destabilized and melts at a lower temperature. (Choice B) is incorrect: only cysteine thiols are oxidized to disulfide crosslinks; a glutamate carboxylate cannot form an -S-S- bond, so no new covalent crosslink is created. (Choice C) is incorrect: backbone amide-to-carbonyl hydrogen bonds define secondary structure and depend on main-chain atoms, which are unchanged by swapping one side chain for another, so this force is not what is lost. (Choice D) is incorrect: proteins are least soluble near their isoelectric point and adding charge can raise solubility, but greater solubility does not itself unfold a protein, and the destabilizing residue here is buried in the core rather than exposed on the surface. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that the hydrophobic effect stabilizes tertiary structure to predict why burying a charged side chain in the core destabilizes the fold.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = 'd34f18b3-ddb8-468f-8477-ab896ffd6dbb';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = 'd34f18b3-ddb8-468f-8477-ab896ffd6dbb';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('d34f18b3-ddb8-468f-8477-ab896ffd6dbb', 'B', 'misconception', 'wrong-side-chain-chemistry'),
  ('d34f18b3-ddb8-468f-8477-ab896ffd6dbb', 'C', 'adjacent_fact', 'secondary-vs-tertiary-confusion'),
  ('d34f18b3-ddb8-468f-8477-ab896ffd6dbb', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'solubility-stability-conflation');

-- [Side-Chain Classification] reengineer ans=B medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Cysteine and methionine are the two amino acids whose R-groups contain a sulfur atom. A student must assign each residue to one of the standard side-chain classes (nonpolar, polar uncharged, acidic, or basic). Which statement correctly describes how each is classified and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Both are polar uncharged, because any side chain containing a sulfur heteroatom is hydrophilic."}, {"label": "B", "text": "Cysteine is polar uncharged and methionine is nonpolar, because only the sulfhydryl group forms a permanent dipole."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Methionine is polar uncharged and cysteine is nonpolar, because the thioether sulfur is the more electronegative group."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Both are nonpolar, because sulfur-containing side chains are too weakly polar to be hydrophilic."}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'B',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' Side chains are sorted into nonpolar, polar uncharged, acidic, or basic classes not by which atoms they contain but by the property those atoms actually confer: the polar uncharged class is defined by heteroatoms (O, S, or N) that form a permanent dipole within the R-group, whereas a side chain whose heteroatom does not create such a dipole stays nonpolar. The two sulfur residues sit on opposite sides of this line: cysteine''s free sulfhydryl (\(-\text{SH}\)) forms a permanent dipole, but methionine''s terminal thioether \"generally cannot form a permanent dipole within the molecule and retains low solubility,\" so methionine \"is usually classified as a nonpolar, hydrophobic amino acid\" (LibreTexts Biochemistry, lines 5257-5258). (Choice A) is incorrect: it assumes that merely containing a sulfur heteroatom makes a side chain hydrophilic, but classification depends on whether that heteroatom forms a permanent dipole, which methionine''s thioether does not. (Choice B) is correct: cysteine''s sulfhydryl forms a permanent dipole and places it in the polar uncharged class alongside serine and threonine (lines 5262-5263), while methionine''s dipole-free thioether keeps it nonpolar. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the two assignments, and no greater electronegativity of the thioether drives polarity — it is the sulfhydryl of cysteine, not the thioether of methionine, that produces the dipole. (Choice D) is incorrect: it overgeneralizes methionine''s nonpolar behavior to cysteine, ignoring that the free \(-\text{SH}\) of cysteine does form a permanent dipole and makes cysteine polar uncharged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the permanent-dipole criterion for the polar uncharged class to two chemically similar residues and infer that identical elemental composition does not guarantee identical classification.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba', 'A', 'misconception', 'presence of a heteroatom equals polarity'),
  ('76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba', 'C', 'reversed_relationship', 'swapped class assignments'),
  ('76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba', 'D', 'misconception', 'all sulfur side chains are nonpolar');

-- [Peptide Bond Formation & Hydrolysis] SCOPE-REPLACE ans=A hard skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'Two free amino acids are joined into a dipeptide. Before the reaction, only the carbonyl (C=O) oxygen of the N-terminal residue''s α-carboxyl group is labeled with the heavy isotope ¹⁸O; that carboxyl group''s hydroxyl (–OH) oxygen and every other oxygen in both amino acids are ordinary ¹⁶O. The N-terminal residue contributes the carboxyl carbon of the new peptide bond, and the C-terminal residue contributes the attacking α-amino group. After the peptide bond forms and the released water molecule is collected, is that water ¹⁸O-labeled, and why?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "No — the water contains only ¹⁶O, because the labeled carbonyl oxygen is retained in the new amide bond while the oxygen that leaves as water comes from the carboxyl group''s ¹⁶O hydroxyl"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Yes — the water is ¹⁸O-labeled, because the carbonyl oxygen leaves as part of the water while the hydroxyl oxygen is retained in the new amide bond"}, {"label": "C", "text": "No — the water contains only ¹⁶O, because its oxygen is supplied by the C-terminal residue''s amino group rather than by either oxygen of the N-terminal carboxyl group"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Yes — the water is ¹⁸O-labeled, because both oxygens of the N-terminal residue''s carboxyl group leave together as part of the single released water molecule"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' A peptide bond forms by dehydration synthesis: the α-amino nitrogen of one amino acid acts as a nucleophile and attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon of the other amino acid''s α-carboxyl group, and "in this reaction, water is released." In this nucleophilic acyl substitution the carbonyl carbon keeps its carbonyl oxygen, which becomes the C=O of the new amide bond, whereas the oxygen expelled as water is the carboxyl group''s hydroxyl (–OH) oxygen — the textbook notes this hydroxyl "must be activated to provide a better leaving group than OH−." Because only the retained carbonyl oxygen carries the ¹⁸O label and the departing hydroxyl oxygen is ¹⁶O, the collected water contains ordinary ¹⁶O. (Choice A) is correct: the labeled carbonyl oxygen stays behind in the amide bond, so the leaving-group oxygen that ends up in water is the unlabeled ¹⁶O hydroxyl oxygen, giving unlabeled water. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the two carboxyl oxygens, claiming the carbonyl oxygen leaves and the hydroxyl is retained; in fact the carbonyl oxygen is retained in the amide and the hydroxyl oxygen departs, so the water is not ¹⁸O-labeled. (Choice C) is incorrect: amino groups contain no oxygen, so the water''s oxygen cannot come from the C-terminal residue''s amino group; the oxygen of the released water is always contributed by the carboxyl group. (Choice D) is incorrect: a single water molecule contains only one oxygen atom, and only one carboxyl oxygen (the hydroxyl) is lost — the carbonyl oxygen is retained — so both carboxyl oxygens do not leave together and the water is not ¹⁸O-labeled. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the nucleophile–electrophile mechanism of peptide bond formation to track which specific oxygen leaves as water in an isotope-labeling scenario.',
  difficulty = 'hard',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '24758af0-ee86-48b3-bd36-e9357747a566';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '24758af0-ee86-48b3-bd36-e9357747a566';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('24758af0-ee86-48b3-bd36-e9357747a566', 'B', 'reversed_relationship', 'swaps which carboxyl oxygen (carbonyl vs. hydroxyl) is retained versus lost as water'),
  ('24758af0-ee86-48b3-bd36-e9357747a566', 'C', 'misconception', 'believes the released water''s oxygen comes from the oxygen-free amino group'),
  ('24758af0-ee86-48b3-bd36-e9357747a566', 'D', 'misconception', 'assumes both carboxyl oxygens leave together in the single released water molecule');

-- [Hydrophobicity & Unique Residues] reengineer ans=A medium skill2
UPDATE questions SET
  question_text = 'A structural biologist engineers a single proline substitution into the middle of an otherwise stable \(\alpha\)-helix and finds that the helix unwinds at that position, introducing a kink. Which property of proline best explains why it destabilizes the helix at this internal site?',
  options = '[{"label": "A", "text": "Its ring-bound nitrogen cannot donate the backbone hydrogen bond a helix requires"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Its bulky nonpolar side chain sterically collides with adjacent residue side chains"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Its positively charged side chain electrostatically repels neighboring backbone groups"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Its extra side-chain hydrogen bonds lock neighboring turns too tightly together"}]'::jsonb,
  correct_answer = 'A',
  explanation = 'This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category ''Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'' An \(\alpha\)-helix is held together by regular backbone hydrogen bonds between each residue''s amide N–H (donor) and a carbonyl oxygen four residues away (acceptor). Because proline''s side chain loops back and bonds to its own \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen, that nitrogen sits in the ring as a secondary amine and lacks the free backbone N–H the helix needs; proline is also sterically locked into a limited backbone conformation, so it cannot adopt the helical geometry. (Choice A) is correct: the textbook states proline''s ring "R-group is unable to participate in H-bonding needed for helix stabilization, and also is sterically locked in one conformation" (LibreTexts 18851-18852), so the missing backbone hydrogen-bond donor is exactly what interrupts the helix. (Choice B) is incorrect: proline''s cyclic side chain is actually compact rather than bulky, and helix breakage arises from the lost backbone N–H and conformational rigidity, not from side-chain steric clashes with neighbors. (Choice C) is incorrect: proline is a nonpolar residue with an uncharged side chain (LibreTexts 5242-5245), so it carries no charge to electrostatically repel backbone groups. (Choice D) is incorrect: this reverses the true relationship—proline removes a backbone hydrogen-bond donor rather than adding side-chain hydrogen bonds, so it loosens rather than over-tightens the helix. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply proline''s unique ring structure to predict its mechanistic effect on \(\alpha\)-helix hydrogen bonding in a novel substitution experiment.',
  difficulty = 'medium',
  cognitive_skill = 2
WHERE id = '6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649';
DELETE FROM question_distractor_metadata WHERE question_id = '6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649';
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype) VALUES
  ('6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649', 'B', 'misconception', 'attributes helix disruption to side-chain steric bulk rather than the missing backbone N-H'),
  ('6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649', 'C', 'misconception', 'treats proline as charged/polar when it is nonpolar and uncharged'),
  ('6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649', 'D', 'reversed_relationship', 'claims proline adds stabilizing H-bonds when it actually removes a backbone donor');

